#nexus [SplitsTree6]

BEGIN SPLITSTREE6;
DIMENSIONS nDataNodes=9 nAlgorithms=5;
PROGRAM version='SplitsTree CE (version 6.3.0-beta, built 19 Mar 2024)';
WORKFLOW creationDate='1710899524937'; [Wed Mar 20 14:52:04 NZDT 2024]
END; [SPLITSTREE6]

[!Candida orthopsilosis haplotypes from Del Olmo et al. (2023), Origin of fungal hybrids with pathogenic potential
from warm seawater environments, Nat Commun 14:6919, DOI 10.1038/s41467-023-42679-4. This dataset was used to
illustrate the SplitsTree App in Huson & Bryant (2024), Nat Methods 21:1773-1774.]

[
Methods:
Analysis was performed using SplitsTree CE 6.0.0_alpha (Huson and Bryant 2006).
The original input consisted of 10 taxa and 10 DNA sequences of length 11,368.
The P Distance method (Hamming 1950) was used (default options) so as to obtain a 10 x 10 distance matrix.
The Median Joining method (Bandelt et al 1999) was used (default options) so as to obtain 14 nodes and 17 edges.

References:
Bandelt et al 1999: HJ Bandelt, P. Forster, and A. Röhl. Median-joining networks for inferring intraspecific phylogenies. Molecular Biology and Evolution, 16:37–48, 1999.
Hamming 1950: Hamming, Richard W. Error detecting and error correcting codes. Bell System Technical Journal. 29 (2): 147–160. MR 0035935, 1950.
Huson and Bryant 2006: D.H. Huson and D. Bryant. Application of phylogenetic networks in evolutionary studies. Molecular Biology and Evolution, 23:254–267, 2006.
]

